      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sandwich, settled in 1637 and incorporated in 1639, is Cape Cod’s oldest town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.sandwichhistoricalsociety.org/stories/sandwich-town-history"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town takes its name from Sandwich, Kent, a seaport in England.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandwich%2C_Massachusetts"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1825, Deming Jarves founded the Boston & Sandwich Glass Factory, sparking a local glass industry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandwich%2C_Massachusetts"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "At its peak, the factory employed hundreds of men, women, and children, and the surrounding community grew around its buildings and a train roundhouse.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.sandwichhistoricalsociety.org/stories/sandwich-town-history"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Passenger trains served Sandwich from the 19th century until 1964, first under the Old Colony Railroad and later the New York, New Haven and Hartford Railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
